00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:00.600
point? And that sounds like the selling point for someone who doesn't like Monopoly.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:02.980
I like Monopoly.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:05.740
Other than me, most people don't like Monopoly.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:12.000
Right, right. But the time limit thing is, is very interesting and is a whole nother conversation because

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.100
I do think. It also could be considered house rules.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:15.460
Um, right, right, right.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:21.840
A hundred percent. I do think it adds a completely other element that Monopoly has never explored before this

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:29.940
moment. But I would say that like, it is, it is a selling point, but surely the idea of any tabletop.

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:34.900
I mean, you see this even now with how many tabletop games just get like really low lift

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:37.320
steam ports of just like, yeah, this is the game.

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:39.800
But like now you can play it on, uh, on steam.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:45.780
It's like the, a lot of people just don't want to deal with the hassle of, of handling the

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:49.720
cards and thinking about the money and how and checking the cards to see how much things

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:51.980
cost and just letting the banker handle that.

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:57.980
I do think that just like how the game shows, uh, the NES versions of the game show games,

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:03.140
the promise was to like, now you can play the game show, not play along with the game show.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:05.500
That is kind of like what Monopoly is thrown down here.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:07.980
Sure. Yeah.

00:16:08.180 --> 00:16:09.920
And now go ahead, Joe.

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:14.020
I think that it's, I played this for the first time ever.

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:21.240
I think me playing co-op or co-op two player was the wrong move because I think that like

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:27.520
what this brings to the table that you, you know, that you wouldn't get from just playing

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:30.480
Monopoly is the like, yeah, let's take on these other opponents.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:31.920
Let's have the computer opponent.

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:36.860
So I think it's like, yeah, it's a way to, I appreciate that they made, that they gave

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:41.420
it a purpose to be a video game where it's like, you know, you know, cause while I played

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:45.080
this with my fiance and we were like, we played for a little while, it was just, just the two

00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:49.440
of us probably should have had a computer in there as well because it went by very fast.

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:53.520
But like at the end of it, she was like, yeah, but you know, why would, why would someone

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:54.800
play this and not just play Monopoly?

00:16:54.800 --> 00:17:00.080
And I think the answer to that is because there's this other element.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:02.120
Now you can take on these other players.

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:06.860
You can, you can set up, you can have these predetermined setups that you got to try and

00:17:06.860 --> 00:17:08.520
like beat little challenges and things like that.

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:12.500
Granted, you could do that yourself, but like it's built in.

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:17.020
I have a fun little anecdote about the computer opponents.

00:17:17.020 --> 00:17:23.680
Um, and at, at first I thought that like they were actually fucking with me, but I think

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:28.260
this is just like how the game, like how they operate within the game.

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:35.180
Like there's no, like it, it, the computer will proposition you like they will try and wheel

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:38.760
and deal like one would in an actual monopoly game.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:48.740
But what I got was a computer opponent suggesting a trade to me, um, where I give them, I don't

00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:55.300
know, uh, New York Avenue and they give me nothing.

00:17:59.020 --> 00:17:59.860
Let me finish. Okay.

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:04.920
All right. So I actually said like, are you shitting me?

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:12.260
Is this game actually saying that it thinks I'm going to just give them the, the deed for

00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:13.360
this, this property.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.720
And then when I just said no, or you know what?

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:17.600
No, I didn't say no.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:20.380
What I did was because I was kind of spiteful.

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:26.660
Uh, I took my property off of there and then I said, why don't you give me your property

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:30.840
for nothing? And then it came back with a price and I'm like, Oh, okay.

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:35.960
This is actually a little bit more, uh, like intuitive here.

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:37.220
So, you know what?

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:39.400
I did buy that property off of them.

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:41.680
Um, but it was very funny.

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:45.380
Like that initial reaction, just that initial vision of them.

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:46.500
Just give me that.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:53.300
Give me it. But in, and that was like that whole element was something that I was worried about when

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:54.380
I first started playing.

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:58.780
Was that like, how are you going to, I don't know, in my head, it was like hard to imagine

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:03.620
being able to do the things that are not on the board in an NES game where it's like,

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:08.620
so I'm going to be able to like, yeah, make a trade or offer money for something or say,

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:09.860
Oh, I'll give you this if you do.

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:12.200
And it like really gives you a lot of those tools.

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:15.340
And I'm not sure why I had so little faith in their ability to do that.

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:19.460
But I was surprised that just like, Oh, I can do like, just like anything I would just,

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:24.320
I would, any kind of deal I would try to make just while sitting across from you at a table.

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:28.500
I can, you know, they, they have something built in here where I can make that offer.

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:34.580
Yeah. This game obviously gets the job of like simulating monopoly, right?

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:36.020
Like, like it has all the spaces.

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:37.260
The game works.

00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:42.260
Like, we're not going to talk about like how, how you purchase deeds or stuff like that.

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:50.380
I think we are blowing by two of what I thought were like unbelievable features for the NES.

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:53.920
And that is both the trading and this time limit thing.

00:19:53.920 --> 00:20:18.880
So if we can start, since we're on the topic of the trading, I was very impressed with this system of the computer being able to both know what it, what it needs slash wants in a, in a trade, but also like that the system wouldn't be designed to just be like the computer can just offer, you know, property for property or property for an obscene amount of money.

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:24.620
Like that, it becomes a negotiation of like, it first goes in and says, I want this property from you.

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:26.500
And then you say, okay, you want that property from me?

00:20:26.660 --> 00:20:27.860
I want $700.

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.660
And then it says, no, not $700.

00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:33.600
If you want it to be $700, you also have to give me.

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:37.160
And then it would like look for either another property or something or lower its money.

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:42.560
I couldn't believe that I was having an actual negotiation with the computer on NES hardware.

00:20:42.820 --> 00:20:45.860
Like I'm totally down with that happening today.

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:54.560
I was very surprised that the trading could be so robust and that I could be having those conversations with the computer opponents as well.

00:20:54.560 --> 00:21:01.460
Because obviously the correct way to play Monopoly, which my family, my own family did not play this way.

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:04.320
And maybe it's just because we were kids and they wanted the game to end sooner.

00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:11.220
But we did not play that you needed to own all of the same color to, in order to start buying houses.

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:12.920
Oh, that I did play.

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:15.860
I think that changes the entire game.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:17.240
It's beyond broken, right?

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:19.780
It's just the world's worst version of Monopoly.

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:25.600
We found out very quickly, or I did when I started reading the rules, that like, hold on a minute, you have to own all the properties.

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:28.960
And as a result, that's what makes the trading system so interesting.

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:35.440
So when you know that you have two of the greens and you need the third one, you go find the computer that has it.

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:40.860
And rather than just say like, hey, you know, I'll give you some other property that I think you need.

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:43.080
You can just say, I want your property.

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:44.120
What do you want for it?

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:51.120
And sometimes it will just make a ridiculous demand of, you know, $1,700 or whatever, because it knows it's got you over the barrel.

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:53.540
But I'm still impressed by that.

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:57.060
That's my question. Because, you know, I didn't play single player like that.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:01.300
I'm curious, does it like, know that you need that?

00:22:01.580 --> 00:22:08.960
And that if you, like, if I have Boardwalk and I'm asking for Park Place, does it think like, hold on a second, then you're going to have a monopoly.

00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:10.800
And like, does it ever say no?

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:15.500
In my experience, it won't even trade you Park Place if you have Boardwalk.

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:20.100
Yeah. Sometimes it shuts down and it just says no.

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:21.660
And then you move on.

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:25.640
Sometimes it will ask for the, like a dollar amount.

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:35.860
I've never, like what you were just describing, Mike, about like actually haggling and then it like offering a property of its own or like, I didn't see that.

00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:47.060
Like, I've actually, like, just to test it a little bit, I offered the same thing twice and it gave me the same price, like dollar value twice.

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:52.320
Right. At no point said like, oh, I'll give you this property and this money or vice versa.

00:22:53.680 --> 00:23:01.780
So, it felt a little bit more limiting than you're describing it as, but it was definitely more than I was expecting.

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:07.160
Yeah. It even went as far as like to offer new properties of mine back to it.

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:25.680
So, like, even though it had started a deal just asking for one of my properties, when I asked for a property that would obviously help me complete my set as part of the next step in the negotiation, it started saying, all right, well, then you've also got to give me $700 and your electrical company ended up a water company.

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:28.760
So, I was like, hold on a minute, you're walking away here with too good of a deal.

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:37.200
And I just find all that very impressive because that is the part that like, I would honestly say like, some humans don't play well.

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:43.140
Like, the trading, they don't understand that they're making a bad trade and they're screwing everybody else over by just handing shit away.

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:45.340
And you're like, you know, you have to have that conversation with them.

00:23:45.460 --> 00:23:56.280
The fact that you can still have those kinds of conversations with the computers, I think the only thing that's truly missing from the computers is, and I can't figure out, you know, how much of this is missing.

00:23:56.340 --> 00:24:03.620
But when you pick computer players, you choose from portraits on the wall and you have to select these characters.

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:05.820
And it seems like...

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:08.160
There's no situation where it shows like, are they aggressive?

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:14.140
Right, right. It seems to imply that there are like four good ones and four bad ones, right?

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:17.940
Like, because it says something about like the bottom half or something and you go to the other side.

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:21.780
So, maybe there's like four expert players and four novice players.

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:29.800
But in the manual, there's things like, you know, if you chose Arthur, Arthur is an orphanage-raised millionaire who drives hard bargains.

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:32.700
And it's like, does that mean that he's a tougher trader?

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:39.940
Like, I didn't put 50 hours into this game to learn the ins and outs of every computer opponent.

00:24:39.940 --> 00:24:41.780
But that would be neat to see.

00:24:41.960 --> 00:24:47.580
I just don't think I was able to see differences like that in these computer personalities.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:25:17.560
I just don't think I was able to see.

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:47.560
I just don't think I was able to see.

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:17.480
I just don't think I was able to see.

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:36.640
So, then the next natural thing to talk about is the time limit.

00:26:36.980 --> 00:26:38.820
And I agree with you, Sean.

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:40.680
Definitely a selling point.

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:42.360
Game changer. Right.

00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:52.900
A game changer for Monopoly because now what it does is you can even set a game to end in as early as 15 minutes all the way to – it was funny.

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:56.740
At first, I thought these were in – like the unit of measurement was in minutes.

00:26:57.140 --> 00:26:58.280
Seconds. So, I was like – yeah.

00:26:58.460 --> 00:26:59.900
I was like 15 seconds.

00:27:00.080 --> 00:27:02.300
Like, is that per turn we only have 15 seconds?

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:06.160
And then I was like, oh, you can set the time to be a four-hour game.

00:27:06.160 --> 00:27:10.800
And then I was actually pretty impressed with thinking about like how does that work on the hardware side?

00:27:10.940 --> 00:27:12.760
Like how does it count down like that?

00:27:12.980 --> 00:27:14.040
Yeah, really. You know, it's pretty impressive.

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:22.260
But since I don't know the answer to that, I'm going to move on and say that the interesting thing was that for my very first game, I chose.

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:23.520
Let's just – let's do it.

00:27:23.600 --> 00:27:24.740
Let's do 15 minutes.

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:29.940
It's like let's see how much of this game I can learn and let's see what happens in 15 minutes.

00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:32.080
And I was very satisfied.

00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:33.860
I only had two computer opponents.

00:27:34.080 --> 00:27:37.000
So, this way there was enough like turns to play and everything.

00:27:37.140 --> 00:27:44.280
But I was very satisfied with a lot of the 15-minute Monopoly experience assuming you just want a quick game of Monopoly.

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:58.720
I think though that it like it actually is – I was only just thinking about this right now in that an actual time limit, like literal seconds counting down while you're playing is way too easy to game.

00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:01.980
And it needs to instead be like a turn limit.

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:07.260
Because if you're in the lead and you're maybe –

00:28:07.260 --> 00:28:20.140
Yeah, you're maybe like just coming up on a stretch of the board that's got all hotels and you don't own any of them, you might just slow boat it a little bit and then immediately win.

00:28:20.300 --> 00:28:26.220
So, it needs to be like – I don't know how many turns it usually takes.

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:33.220
But in like divisions of that many, it should be that instead of seconds and minutes.

00:28:33.780 --> 00:28:36.540
Yeah, maybe even steps.

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:39.060
How many spaces have you moved?

00:28:39.820 --> 00:28:47.380
Mm-hmm. And then obviously the way that the time limit works is that at the end, it does your total like net worth.

00:28:47.380 --> 00:28:53.860
So, your assets plus your cash on hand minus any mortgages you have and stuff like that.

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:59.340
But I just think like this is such a interesting way.

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:10.520
And I guess like technically a game like Mario Party was doing something like this where like you say how many turns you want to play and it just says like, okay, after 20 turns, whoever has the most stars wins.

00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:14.400
But like the idea of like even – we don't have to do time limits.

00:29:14.420 --> 00:29:16.100
We could do like a net worth limit.

00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:18.480
First one to hit $5,000 or something.

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:20.040
That'd be nice, yeah. That's the winner.

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:24.160
You know, there's so much – there's such a different way of play here.

00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:33.660
And I'll admit I was naive enough to not game the system on these time limit plays to just like run down the clock because one, what's the fucking point?

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:34.880
I'm just playing against myself.

00:29:34.880 --> 00:29:36.400
You also don't see the clock.

00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:37.560
So, that's helpful.

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:38.980
You can check the clock though.

00:29:38.980 --> 00:29:43.640
You can in the menu screen when it's your turn.

00:29:43.820 --> 00:29:45.180
Okay. You can check how much time is left.

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:55.880
But all those things aside, I do think that just like this was just a huge breakthrough I had not considered in Monopoly before.

00:29:56.120 --> 00:29:58.940
But it's like an of course kind of – like of course time limit.

00:29:59.160 --> 00:30:01.060
These games are so freaking long.

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:03.180
Finally, only an hour.

00:30:04.900 --> 00:30:06.000
That's how I'd do it.

00:30:06.380 --> 00:30:14.360
You last an hour. You could technically – like you could play regular Monopoly and just set an alarm on your phone.

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:17.540
Yeah. Like, you know, problem solved.

00:30:17.740 --> 00:30:21.940
But it's not on the board or in the game.

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:24.700
So, like that is a feature.

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:26.660
It's a feature now. Yeah.

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:33.140
And like I think that there's also like features here that I never thought of that you could easily do in Monopoly.

00:30:33.140 --> 00:30:40.920
But I just never considered it in there built into this game like the time limit or like starting with a certain setup.

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:45.320
Your opponent already starts with these properties and houses on them, things like that.

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:48.240
Seeing what you could do. I don't know how –

00:30:48.240 --> 00:30:49.120
Scenarios. Yeah.

00:30:49.120 --> 00:30:52.380
I don't know how like much you can really do.

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:53.900
Like it's still just luck based.

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:58.060
It's just like harder to dig yourself out of a hole, I guess, with some of these scenarios.

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:00.660
But I do find it an interesting idea.

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:05.520
Yeah. I mean, you can edit nearly everything about the game of Monopoly.

00:31:05.880 --> 00:31:10.040
Like I actually thought it was going to let me change the board titles at one point.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:15.840
Like I was going to be able to like go in there and edit the board and be like, no, this space should actually be called Michael Avenue.

00:31:16.120 --> 00:31:22.040
Like I could have sworn they were going to let me do that because you can change how much money each person has down to the dollar.

00:31:22.040 --> 00:31:28.400
So naturally I gave myself one more dollar than the computers just to give myself that extra privilege.

00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:35.400
But yeah, cash, properties, which tokens, how many buildings are available.

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:40.020
All these things can be changed from the game editor before you start.

00:31:40.020 --> 00:31:45.260
And then, yes, those preset scenarios, too, which wasn't something I was like understanding at first.

00:31:45.360 --> 00:31:47.040
It was like I was like, what's the big boys?

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:48.840
Is that like what's big boy mode?

00:31:49.060 --> 00:31:52.500
Like I just I wasn't like are the pieces going to be bigger?

00:31:52.500 --> 00:32:09.040
Like I wasn't sure what was going on there, but the fact that you can edit all this stuff really does introduce like an amazing single player experience to to someone who it would be worth it for them to purchase Monopoly on the NES.

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:14.240
Because like the board game is there for when friends and family want to play Monopoly.

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:20.020
The person who loves Monopoly and can't get enough of it needs an NES game.

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:22.320
Is never invited over. Yeah, exactly.

00:32:22.500 --> 00:32:23.340
Never. Yeah.

00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:32.540
One always in their basement, but like needs these kinds of features to like justify having Monopoly in a video game format.

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:37.440
But, you know, there's also like for the person who loves Monopoly, the person who doesn't love Monopoly.

00:32:38.140 --> 00:32:40.960
Shockingly, again, a drop in drop out feature.

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:45.620
You can in the middle of your game shift your computer in the middle of your game.

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:46.620
You can go to the menu.

00:32:46.780 --> 00:32:49.380
You can shift your computer to a human player and vice versa.

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:51.580
If your friends like I'm done with Monopoly.

00:32:51.580 --> 00:32:53.380
Okay, fine. I'll shift you to a computer player.

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:55.760
Can I tell you something really scummy about that?

00:32:56.080 --> 00:33:01.760
What? Me and my siblings somehow came up with this strategy for Mario Party.

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:03.860
And sorry for all the Mario Party stuff.

00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:05.920
But I mean, like, come on. This is board game.

00:33:05.920 --> 00:33:07.500
This is millennial Monopoly.

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:18.020
