00:49:12.920 --> 00:49:20.640
It's all we know. Trying to put things maybe closer to the date of the release of the NES game, though.

00:49:20.840 --> 00:49:27.980
Maybe some other things that you guys would like to know that was going on was Mikhail Gorbachev visited Minnesota in 1990.

00:49:28.980 --> 00:49:34.280
And Parker Brothers, which was then part of Minnesota-based Tonka Corporation.

00:49:34.280 --> 00:49:35.820
You know, the people who make the trucks.

00:49:36.380 --> 00:49:38.460
The toy trucks, not the real trucks.

00:49:38.500 --> 00:49:39.420
Best trucks. Right.

00:49:39.880 --> 00:49:52.140
Now, what they did to honor him showing up to Minnesota was they made a Russian language Monopoly, which is still available to view at the Minnesota Historical Society.

00:49:53.700 --> 00:49:57.860
So if anybody lives near Minnesota and wants to take a picture for us, I would love to see it.

00:49:58.540 --> 00:50:01.020
Absolutely. You know where else Monopoly's been?

00:50:01.380 --> 00:50:03.620
Tell me. It's not where.

00:50:03.960 --> 00:50:04.940
It's what.

00:50:04.940 --> 00:50:10.360
Monopoly has also been a game show on ABC.

00:50:11.400 --> 00:50:13.360
No. This is crazy.

00:50:14.140 --> 00:50:15.040
When was this?

00:50:16.540 --> 00:50:21.380
Joe, I'm so glad you asked how it works, but I also have to acknowledge that Sean asked when was it.

00:50:21.500 --> 00:50:28.760
Because it was. It ran basically for all of like three months from June to September of 1990.

00:50:29.760 --> 00:50:30.580
Wow. But that's again.

00:50:30.860 --> 00:50:34.040
Look, it's gearing up around this time where the NES game is.

00:50:34.040 --> 00:50:38.060
So there was clearly some, you know, Monopoly was being revitalized.

00:50:38.400 --> 00:50:49.880
But it aired on 12 Saturdays right after Super Jeopardy, which I don't know if that was like another show separate from Jeopardy, if they were just calling Jeopardy Super Jeopardy.

00:50:49.880 --> 00:50:51.980
Did Alex Trebek have multiple mustaches?

00:50:52.420 --> 00:50:54.400
Yeah. Was he on the Super Nintendo?

00:50:54.400 --> 00:50:58.480
No. Here's what's interesting about the Monopoly game.

00:50:58.600 --> 00:51:01.380
So it had a giant Monopoly board.

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:06.000
And then it had like three contestants like stand over the board.

00:51:06.140 --> 00:51:07.340
But no pieces.

00:51:07.680 --> 00:51:10.340
Nobody picked like their symbol.

00:51:10.720 --> 00:51:12.460
Nobody moved around the board.

00:51:12.460 --> 00:51:16.540
Instead, the board, you just went to each place on it.

00:51:16.820 --> 00:51:20.060
And you would answer a crossword style clue.

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:24.820
And if you got the clue correct, you would win the property.

00:51:25.420 --> 00:51:29.060
And so the first property colors are brown.

00:51:29.600 --> 00:51:31.100
So if somebody.

00:51:31.100 --> 00:51:33.200
Already too much skill involved for this.

00:51:33.200 --> 00:51:47.940
Right. Okay. If somebody gets the first property in the brown suite, but then the other person gets the other place, then they have to answer a face off question to determine who wins the Monopoly, who gets the set.

00:51:48.620 --> 00:51:50.460
That continues on for all of them.

00:51:50.600 --> 00:51:54.740
So if all three contestants won a property on each of the green spaces, they would eat.

00:51:54.860 --> 00:51:56.140
All three of them would face off.

00:51:56.300 --> 00:52:00.420
Or you wouldn't have to worry about the face off if you just got all three correct to begin with.

00:52:00.960 --> 00:52:02.960
Okay. I'm not sure.

00:52:02.960 --> 00:52:04.480
I don't know why that had to exist.

00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:08.440
Yeah. I kind of want to watch an episode, but.

00:52:08.940 --> 00:52:14.560
Yeah. And in other pop culture stuff, Sean, we went to Atlantic City.

